Make the simulated gimbal hold the axes it is told to lock.

PX4's simulated gimbal writes the commanded angles straight onto Gazebo joints
that ride on the airframe:

    src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp, Run()
        if (pollSetpoint()) {
                //TODO handle device flags
                publishJointCommand(...)

That TODO is the whole of it. The device flags are stored and never read, so
every command runs as a vehicle-relative angle whatever the flags say, and the
simulated gimbal is a follow mode device on all three axes. A real MAVLink
gimbal given ROLL_LOCK and PITCH_LOCK holds the horizon while the aircraft
banks under it, which is what the Chimera flight code asks for on every command
it sends (`umd_uas/gimbal.py`, flags 12).

The gap is not small. At 20 degrees of bank the simulated camera rolled 20
degrees with the airframe, so a level horizon in the picture was a lie, an image
overlay slid off the video, and a pixel cast at the ground landed wrong by the
whole bank angle. Nothing reported an error.

TWO THINGS HAVE TO BE RIGHT. The frame the setpoint is given in, and the order
the arms turn in. The first is arithmetic anyone would look for. The second is
the one that bites.

ONE: THE FRAME.

The lock bits name the frame the setpoint is given in.

    YAW_LOCK                      earth frame
    ROLL_LOCK or PITCH_LOCK only  vehicle frame: the earth frame turned by
                                  the heading of the airframe, and level
    no lock bit                   the airframe itself

The joints turn against the airframe, so the airframe attitude has to come off
the setpoint before the angles are written. `px4-gzgimbal-frame.patch` already
named those three frames, for the report:

    report:  q_report = conj(q_reference) * q_camera

This patch is the same arithmetic the other way round, for the command:

    command: q_joint  = conj(q_vehicle) * q_reference * q_setpoint

With no lock bit the reference frame is the airframe, the two conjugates cancel,
and the joints take the setpoint unchanged. So a follow mode command behaves
exactly as it did before this patch, and only a locked axis changes.

TWO: THE ORDER THE ARMS TURN IN.

`modules/sim/scenes/models/gimbal/model.sdf` builds the mount as three revolute
joints in a chain: the vertical arm about the yaw axis, then the horizontal arm
about the roll axis, then the camera about the pitch axis. The attitude those
arms build is therefore

    R = Rz(yaw) * Rx(roll) * Ry(pitch)

and the angles that build a wanted attitude have to be read back out of it in
that same order.

`matrix::Eulerf` reads `Rz * Ry * Rx`, the aerospace order, which puts pitch
before roll. **The two orders give the same three angles only while roll is
zero.** Every command this simulator ever saw had roll zero: PX4 commands roll
zero by default, and the Chimera flight code sends a level horizon on every
command. So GZGimbal has always read the setpoint in the wrong order and it has
never once mattered. The first thing to ask these arms for a roll was the frame
arithmetic above.

Reading a stabilization setpoint in the aerospace order and writing it onto
these arms misses by up to 40 degrees across the flight envelope, and it misses
most where the camera is pitched furthest down, which is where this camera
spends its time. It is not a gain error and it does not halve or trim away: with
the camera pitched down, a rotation about the roll arm swings the boresight in
azimuth rather than rolling the picture, because the roll arm turns before the
camera is pitched. That was measured on the running simulator before it was
derived: a commanded roll of 2.43 degrees moved the boresight 2.05 degrees in
azimuth and left the roll almost unchanged.

So the patch reads the joint angles in the chain's own order, in
`jointAnglesFromAttitude()`, and both the locked path and the plain path use it.
Checked over 20000 random airframe attitudes with the setpoint the flight code
sends: the aerospace order leaves up to 40.096 degrees of pointing error, and
the chain order leaves 1.7e-06 degrees.

The yaw arm never has to move for any of it. For a setpoint of roll zero and
vehicle-frame yaw zero, the yaw joint this decomposition asks for is zero at
every airframe attitude, to 8e-15 degrees. A mount with a roll arm and a pitch
arm holds the horizon exactly, and takes its azimuth from the airframe, which is
what the Chimera mount is built to do.

WHAT ELSE CHANGES.

1. Stabilization runs every cycle, not only when a setpoint arrives. A locked
   axis is held against the world, so its joint angle depends on where the
   airframe is now. A real device stabilizes continuously from its own IMU. The
   joints are only republished while an axis is locked, so a follow mode command
   and the RC path keep their old timing exactly.

2. The RC path clears the flags and the stored attitude. It commands the joints
   themselves, so there is no frame to hold anything against.

3. GIMBAL_DEVICE_INFORMATION now describes this mount. It gains HAS_ROLL_LOCK
   and HAS_PITCH_LOCK, because the device now really does hold those axes, and
   it loses HAS_YAW_AXIS, HAS_YAW_FOLLOW and SUPPORTS_INFINITE_YAW, because
   model.sdf gives the yaw joint equal limits and holds the arm at zero. The
   reported yaw range becomes 0 to 0 for the same reason, which also clamps the
   joint command to what the joint can do rather than to plus and minus
   infinity.

   Nothing in PX4 reads these flags; they are what a consumer reads to learn the
   mount. `umd_uas/gimbal.py` subscribes to the report and says out loud when
   the mount declares no yaw axis while its yaw limits allow one, so the axis
   count is read from the device on both the simulator and the aircraft rather
   than assumed. If the Chimera mount is ever shown to have a yaw actuator,
   three things open together: the yaw joint limits in model.sdf, the yaw
   capability flags and range here, and `yaw_angle_min` and `yaw_angle_max` in
   the flight code parameters.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO.

It does not make the gimbal hold a region of interest. PX4 computes the pointing
for one once, when the command arrives (`output_mavlink.cpp`, OutputMavlinkV2
recomputes only on new setpoints), and that is PX4 rather than the simulator: a
real vehicle with a MAVLink v2 gimbal behaves the same way. Holding a place is
the flight code's work, and `umd_uas/gimbal.py` does it by re-sending the
attitude.

It does not add a yaw actuator. An azimuth the mount cannot reach still needs
the aircraft to turn.

It does not touch the joint controllers. Each one is effort limited at
cmd_max 0.3 with p_gain 0.8, so a fast bank is still followed with a lag. That
is a rate limit, not a pointing error.

Apply after px4-gzgimbal-frame.patch, which this one builds on: it uses
vehicleAttitude() and attitudeReferenceFrame() from that patch. From
